nodecode-degeneracy-guard is a rewrite of simin-yuan's simin-yuan/hermes-degeneracy-guard:

  https://github.com/simin-yuan/hermes-degeneracy-guard
  commit ffc28d0ab9ac6b6c0cbb118ed5fb0c06909a3dc1
  Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Simin (Simon) Yuan (LICENSE, NOTICE)

Rewritten: the whole detector (loop_detector.py) -- the three criteria, the
data-shape recognisers, the prose test and the tool-argument rule with its
thresholds, in detector.lisp -- and the parts of __init__.py,
tool_arg_guard.py, session_length_guard.py and paths.py a cell can carry: the
local incident record under the home, the ask-the-person gate with Allow once
and Deny, the exemption by tool name, and the long-session notice with its
de-bounce. The frozen thresholds keep their original numbers.

Left out, and why: the hooks Hermes has and Nodecode does not.
on_stream_delta scored the text stream chunk by chunk and could not abort
generation; transform_llm_output swapped a flagged turn's output for a notice;
Nodecode has no point on the stream, so the stream detector survives as
check-text, answered by the degeneracy_check tool with kind "output" and by
/degeneracy check, and the replacement notice is gone with the capability
that was missing anyway. post_llm_call's bookkeeping is unnecessary because
the :TOOL hook is the one that asks. The Hermes human-approval return value
({"action": "approve", "rule_key": ...}, a per-tool "always") became the
core's own ASK-OPERATOR question, which offers Allow once or Deny and nothing
sticky, so an "always" for a tool is the section's allow_tools instead. The
session-length guard counted conversation messages; a cell reads the model
requests a session has made at the :ROUND point, which is the same growth
seen from where a cell can see it, and it reports that number.
